relationship between Minoan, Mycenaean, and classical Greek civilisation. Childe
emphasises Evans’s thesis that classical Greek civilisation is rooted in Minoan 
culture and its continental daughter, Mycenaean civilisation, and notes Evans’s 
repeated use of prehistoric remains to illuminate Greek religion and life. The 
review confirms the publication’s bibliographic details and recognises the Ring 
of Nestor as a significant monument in arguments about Minoan influence on 
classical Greek religious conceptions and myth.",
      "source_ref": "s2"
    },
    {
      "local_ref": "d3",
      "document_summary": "The book chapter \"The Ring of Nestor and the Quest 
for Authenticity\" in the Aegis 18 volume Current Approaches and New 
Perspectives in Aegean Iconography, authored by A. G. Vlachopoulos, offers an 
extended re-examination of the Ring of Nestor. It focuses on questions of 
authenticity, style, and iconography, treating the ring as a problematic Aegean 
glyptic object whose genuine Late Bronze Age origin has been doubted. Within the
broader framework of changing views of style and iconography in Aegean glyptic, 
the chapter reassesses Evans’s original dating and cultural attribution and 
situates the Ring of Nestor among other contested or dubious artifacts. It 
examines methodological criteria for determining authenticity and evaluates the 
ring’s imagery and stylistic features against securely provenanced Minoan and 
Mycenaean examples.",
