discusses specific motifs on the ring, including a guardian figure sometimes 
described as a watch-dog or dragon and a tree-of-the-world motif, and compares 
these to parallels in authenticated Minoan glyptic and wall paintings. The paper
reviews earlier interpretations that saw the scene as a goddess epiphany or 
ritual and reconsiders whether the unusual combination of motifs fits within 
known Late Bronze Age Aegean iconographic patterns. By classifying the ring as a
\"Gemma dubitanda\" (doubtful gem), the article highlights how uncertain 
archaeological provenance and atypical iconography complicate firm attribution 
to either Minoan or Mycenaean cultural spheres.",
