    {
      "local_ref": "d1",
      "document_summary": "John Boardman’s Journal of Hellenic Studies article 
examines the object known as the ‘Ring of Nestor’ within a sepulchral treasure 
of gold signet rings and bead seals reportedly found at Thisbe in Boeotia. The 
paper situates the ring among an assemblage of engraved Minoan-style objects, 
discusses its discovery and association with a burial context, and analyzes the 
ring’s engraved scene in detail. Boardman interprets the imagery as a complex 
ritual or afterworld composition involving multiple figures, architectural or 
structural elements, and possibly vegetal or pillar motifs, arguing that it 
provides insight into Minoan conceptions of the afterlife. He addresses 
chronological and cultural issues, proposing that the ring is stylistically Late
Minoan I and thus Cretan in origin, despite its mainland Boeotian findspot, and 
explores implications for trade, exchange, and the movement or heirloom status 
of such objects.",
      "source_ref": "s1"
    },
    {
      "local_ref": "d2",
      "document_summary": "The ResearchGate entry provides access to John 
Boardman’s article on the ‘Ring of Nestor’ and includes an abstract and 
bibliographic information. It notes the Ashmolean Museum in Oxford as the 
institution holding the ring and records that the ring was placed back on 
display there in 1995. The description indicates that the article contains a 
comprehensive catalogue of published seals featuring hybrid creatures, 
underscoring the ring’s importance for the study of Minoan gold signet rings and
Aegean glyptic. The entry implies that Boardman’s text includes technical 
details such as measurements and material description of the ring, although 
these are contained in the attached PDF rather than in the listing itself.",
      "source_ref": "s2"
    },
    {
      "local_ref": "d3",
      "document_summary": "The short Nature note reports that Sir Arthur Evans 
presented his collection of Minoan sealstones, gems, gold rings, and jewellery 
to the Ashmolean Museum in Oxford as an accession to the museum’s ‘Minoan Room’.
It highlights the inclusion of the ‘famous great gold ring of Nestor’ among 
these gifts, along with a group of gold beads known as the ‘Treasure of Thisbe’,
all engraved with scenes of ritual, combat, and ancient legend. The article 
emphasizes the uniqueness and renown of Evans’s collection and states that the 
Ashmolean’s Minoan antiquities became among the most important and complete 
outside Crete, thereby documenting the ring’s provenance as part of Evans’s 
donated holdings and its association with the Treasure of Thisbe.",
      "source_ref": "s3"
    },
    {
      "local_ref": "d4",
      "document_summary": "The Academia.edu paper ‘Some observations on the 
iconography of the “Ring of Nestor”’ focuses on the imagery and stylistic 
