    {
      "local_ref": "d1",
      "document_summary": "This digitized monograph by Sir Arthur Evans, 
published in London in 1925, is a detailed archaeological and iconographic study
centered on the so‑called \"Ring of Nestor\" and a group of related gold signet 
rings and bead seals said to come from a sepulchral treasure at Thisbê (Thisbe) 
in Boeotia, central Greece. The work is structured in two parts: the first 
describes the sepulchral treasure of gold signet rings and bead seals from 
Thisbê, Boeotia, and the second offers an interpretive essay titled \"A glimpse 
into the Minoan after-world\". Evans presents the ring and associated objects as
a funerary deposit, providing descriptions, illustrations, and plates that 
document their form and decoration. He interprets the ring within a Minoan 
cultural and religious framework, associating its imagery and context with 
Minoan civilization, while also connecting it to the mythological figure Nestor.
The monograph is treated as archaeological literature and includes multiple 
plates (Tafeln) depicting the ring and related artifacts.",
      "source_ref": "s1"
    },
    {
      "local_ref": "d2",
      "document_summary": "This 2019 academic article by Hudler reassesses the 
gold signet rings reportedly from Thisbe, Boeotia, with particular focus on the 
so‑called \"Ring of Nestor\" and on arguments about authenticity and provenance.
The study revisits Arthur Evans's original account of a group of gold signet 
rings and bead seals allegedly originating from a tomb at Thisbe in Boeotia and 
his selection of one elaborate gold ring as the \"Ring of Nestor\". Hudler 
emphasizes that the assemblage was acquired via the art market rather than 
recovered in a documented excavation and highlights the problematic nature of 
its claimed sepulchral context. The article situates the find in central Greece 
(Thisbê, Boeotia) and underlines the unusual presence of Minoan‑style 
iconography on a ring said to come from that region. It surveys and discusses 
scholarly doubts about the authenticity of the Ring of Nestor, outlining 
arguments that it may be a genuine Late Bronze Age artifact versus those that 
treat it as a modern forgery, and addresses broader issues surrounding 
unprovenanced Aegean Bronze Age objects.",
      "source_ref": "s2"
    },
    {
      "local_ref": "d3",
      "document_summary": "This 2013 scholarly article by Theodore Eliopoulos, 
published in the ANNUARIO della Scuola Archeologica di Atene e delle Missioni 
Italiane in Oriente, offers a focused analysis of the iconography of the 
so‑called \"Ring of Nestor\" within the context of Aegean Bronze Age art and the
wider problem of forgeries. The abstract states that the paper analyzes the 
imagery on the Ring of Nestor and discusses its significance in both Minoan and 
Mycenaean art traditions, while engaging explicitly with debates over the ring's
authenticity. Eliopoulos notes that generations of scholars have debated the 
genuineness of both \"Nestor's\" and \"Minos'\" rings, placing this ring among a
group of contested Aegean signet rings. A key argument advanced is that the 
depiction of incurved altars on the ring, in combination with other elements 
such as trees and possibly boats, supports the ring's authenticity because these
motifs correspond to known Minoan religious scenes. The article stresses that 
contradictory results from technical analyses make detailed iconographic study 
crucial and compares the ring's motifs with securely provenanced Minoan and 
Mycenaean artworks as part of the authenticity assessment.",
      "source_ref": "s3"
    },
    {
      "local_ref": "d4",
      "document_summary": "This HathiTrust catalog record provides bibliographic
information for Arthur Evans's 1925 monograph titled \"The ring of Nestor; a 
glimpse into the Minoan after-world, and a sepulchral treasure of gold 
signet-rings and bead-seals from Thisbê, Boeotia\". It confirms the full title, 
attributes the work to Sir Arthur Evans, and lists London and the year 1925 as 
the publication details. Subject headings associate the work with Nestor from 
Greek mythology and with Minoan civilization, indicating that Evans connected 
the ring's imagery to both mythological Nestor and Minoan religious contexts. 
The record notes that the volume contains illustrations and plates, implying 
that visual documentation of the ring and related artifacts is included. 
Overall, the entry situates Evans's study within library cataloging frameworks 
and confirms its basic descriptive and thematic information.",
      "source_ref": "s4"
    },
    {
      "local_ref": "d5",
      "document_summary": "This chapter, titled \"Fakes and Dubitanda\" in the 
Cambridge University Press volume The Art and Archaeology of the Aegean Bronze 
Age, discusses issues of authenticity and questionable artifacts within Aegean 
Bronze Age art. Within this broader framework, it references the \"Ring of 
Nestor\" as an example, describing its characteristic iconography as a composite
religious scene featuring a female individual on a boat, altars, sacred trees, 
and baetyls, associated with a divine theme. By drawing comparisons between this
ring and another artifact that displays similar motifs, the chapter situates the
Ring of Nestor among objects whose authenticity has been debated. The context of
the chapter indicates that the Ring of Nestor is treated as a notable case study
in discussions of possible fakes or dubitanda, and that the evaluation of its 
imagery and provenance forms part of scholarly criteria used to distinguish 
genuine from forged or dubious Aegean Bronze Age artworks.",
      "source_ref": "s5"
    }
  ],
