- Title: “The Ring of Nestor and the quest for authenticity” (in F. Blakolmer 
(ed.), Current Approaches and New Perspectives in Aegean Iconography, Aegis 18, 
2020)  
- URL: 
https://www.academia.edu/43416584/_2020_A_G_Vlachopoulos_The_Ring_of_Nestor_and_
the_quest_for_authenticity_in_F_Blakolmer_ed_Current_Approaches_and_New_Perspect
ives_in_Aegean_Iconography_Aegis_18_2020_pp_223_252  
- Author/Publisher: A. G. Vlachopoulos; Academia.edu hosts offprint of chapter  
- Date: 2020  
- Source type: Academic book chapter (Aegean iconography)  
- Triggering query: “Ring of Nestor authenticity 'Gemma dubitanda' Vlachopoulos 
Hudler Oxford Journal of Archaeology 2002”  
- Relevance: Direct, major modern reassessment of authenticity, dating, style 
and iconography; explicitly discusses “Gemmae dubitandae”.  
- Key excerpts/summary:  
  - Notes that the Ring of Nestor has long been treated as a “gemma dubitanda” 
and undertakes a “thorough critical approach” to its authenticity, style and 
iconography in the broader context of problematic early 20th‑c. Aegean glyptic 
pieces.  
  - Reviews Evans’s original publication and subsequent scholarly reception, 
contrasting Evans’s confidence in a Late Bronze Age funerary, Minoan‑related 
attribution with later doubts about provenance, stylistic anomalies, and 
technical features.  
  - Situates the ring among a corpus of “pseudo‑Minoan” or doubtful glyptic 
artifacts and argues that the combination of unusual iconographic motifs, lack 
of secure archaeological context, and technical concerns make its 
straightforward attribution to genuine Minoan or Mycenaean production 
problematic.  
  - Concludes that while some details could be inspired by genuine Aegean or 
Egyptian imagery, the overall composition and context justify maintaining the 
ring’s status as a doubtful object rather than a securely authenticated Late 
Bronze Age artifact.  

2. Journal of Ancient Egyptian Interconnections – “The Pseudo‑Minoan Nestor Ring
and Its Egyptian Iconography”  
- Title: “The Pseudo-Minoan Nestor Ring and Its Egyptian Iconography”  
- URL: https://journals.librarypublishing.arizona.edu/jaei/article/id/960  
- Author/Publisher: (Journal of Ancient Egyptian Interconnections, University of
Arizona) – author name in article, likely Ingo Pini or another specialist (full 
text needed for exact attribution)  
- Date: after 2000 (exact year in article; journal issue provides it)  
- Source type: Peer‑reviewed journal article  
- Triggering query: “\"Ring of Nestor\" Oxford Journal of Archaeology 2002”  
- Relevance: Provides independent re‑evaluation of the ring, explicitly calling 
it “pseudo‑Minoan” and analyzing its iconography in relation to Egyptian motifs.
- Key excerpts/summary:  
  - States that the so‑called Nestor Ring was found in 1924 and “was accepted as
authentic by Sir Arthur Evans whereas many other scholars condemned it as a 
forgery,” framing the piece as “pseudo‑Minoan.”  
  - Examines the ring’s imagery in detail and argues that much of its 
iconography derives from Egyptian models, suggesting that the composition 
reflects modern or hybrid creation rather than genuine Late Bronze Age Aegean 
glyptic traditions.  
  - Emphasizes the problematic provenance and lack of secure archaeological 
documentation, reinforcing its classification among doubtful or forged Aegean 
glyptic objects.  
  - Uses the case to discuss broader issues of forgery, cross‑cultural 
iconographic borrowing, and the need for caution in employing unprovenanced 
artifacts as evidence for Bronze Age religious beliefs.  

3. Wiley – Oxford Journal of Archaeology article “The ‘Ring of Nestor’”  
- Title: “The ‘Ring of Nestor’”  
- URL: https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/abs/10.1111/1468-0092.00048  
- Author/Publisher: Ingo Pini; Oxford Journal of Archaeology (Wiley)  
- Date: 1998 (journal metadata notes vol. 17(1), often cited as 1998; some 
catalogs give 2002 reprint)  
- Source type: Peer‑reviewed journal article  
- Triggering query: “\"Ring of Nestor\" Oxford Journal of Archaeology 2002”  
- Relevance: Key modern technical study; reassesses authenticity and provides 
evidence that supports authenticity in some respects, creating an important 
counterweight to “forgery” claims.  
- Key excerpts/summary:  
  - Described as presenting “new technical observations … which serve to support
the authenticity of the Ring of Nestor.” The study focuses on gemological and 
technical aspects (goldwork, engraving technique, wear, etc.).  
  - Compares the ring’s manufacture with securely provenanced Late Bronze Age 
Aegean gold rings and seals, arguing that several technical features are 
difficult to reproduce in modern forgeries, thus favoring authenticity.  
  - Acknowledges the ring’s problematic provenance and unusual iconography but 
maintains that, on technical grounds, the object may well be a genuine Late 
Bronze Age artifact, though its exact cultural attribution (Minoan vs Mycenaean)
remains debated.  
  - The article is frequently cited by later scholars (e.g., Vlachopoulos, 
Palmer) as a central contribution to the authenticity debate, providing a 
nuanced, partially supportive position rather than uncritical acceptance.  

