5. HathiTrust – Evans’s original monograph  
- Title: “The ring of Nestor; a glimpse into the Minoan after-world, and a 
sepulchral treasure of gold signet-rings and bead-seals from Thisbê, Boeotia”  
- URL: https://catalog.hathitrust.org/Record/000609034  
- Author/Publisher: Sir Arthur Evans; London publisher (1925)  
- Date: 1925  
- Source type: Scholarly monograph (primary publication of the ring and 
associated finds)  
- Triggering query: “Ring of Nestor Oxford Journal of Archaeology 2002”  
- Relevance: Baseline early interpretation and claim of authenticity; needed to 
contrast with later “Gemma dubitanda” classification.  
- Key excerpts/summary:  
  - Evans presents the Ring of Nestor as part of a “sepulchral treasure” from 
Thisbê in Boeotia, associating it with a funerary context and dating it to the 
Late Bronze Age (Mycenaean period) with strong “Minoan” stylistic influences.  
  - Interprets the ring’s complex imagery (goddess or divine figure, tree, 
ritual or epiphany scene) as a “glimpse into the Minoan after‑world,” using it 
to argue for continuity between Minoan religious concepts and later Greek 
beliefs.  
  - Accepts the ring’s authenticity and provenance with little reservation, 
relying on the reported circumstances of discovery and stylistic parallels 
rather than rigorous technical or contextual verification.  
  - Evans’s framing heavily influenced early 20th‑century discussions of Minoan 
roots of Greek religion, which later scholarship has reassessed in light of 
doubts about the ring’s genuineness and context.  

6. Academia.edu – Eliopoulos, “Some Observations on the Iconography of the ‘Ring
of Nestor’”  
- Title: “SOME OBSERVATIONS ON THE ICONOGRAPHY OF THE ‘RING OF NESTOR’”  
- URL: 
https://www.academia.edu/83658257/SOME_OBSERVATIONS_ON_THE_ICONOGRAPHY_OF_THE_RI
NG_OF_NESTOR_  
- Author/Publisher: Theodore Eliopoulos; ANNUARIO della Scuola Archeologica di 
Atene e delle Missioni Italiane in Oriente (2013)  
- Date: 2013  
- Source type: Peer‑reviewed article in an archaeological annual (hosted on 
Academia.edu)  
- Triggering query: “Ring of Nestor Oxford Journal of Archaeology 2002”  
- Relevance: Modern iconographic study explicitly discussing authenticity and 
classification as “gemma dubitanda”; complements technical studies by focusing 
on imagery and stylistic anomalies.  
- Key excerpts/summary:  
  - Analyzes the ring’s iconography within the context of Aegean forgeries and 
authenticity debates, revisiting key features such as the guardian figure, tree 
motif and altar forms.  
  - Notes that contradictions and gaps in technical analyses make iconographic 
study crucial for assessing authenticity; the combination of motifs is described
as unusual or anomalous relative to securely provenanced Minoan/Mycenaean 
glyptic.  
  - Indicates that the ring has been treated as a “gemma dubitanda” and argues 
that its imagery may reflect cultural practices, artistic trends or hybrid 
reconstructions from the early 20th century rather than genuine Bronze Age 
religious scenes.  
  - Emphasizes that both Nestor’s and Minos’ rings have generated long scholarly
debates over genuineness, and calls for cautious, evidence-based treatment of 
such unprovenanced artifacts in reconstructing ancient belief systems.
