{"query": {"id": "20260628180511432543_06285b20", "handle": "q2", "text": "“Ring
of Nestor” physical description iconography gold ring imagery inscription museum
catalogue", "importance": "core"}, "items": [{"id": 
"20260628180640348265_01d4d8e2", "type": "Evidence", "text": "The article’s 
title describes the Ring of Nestor as “A Glimpse into the Minoan After-World and
a Sepulchral Treasure of Gold Signet-Rings and Bead-Seals from Thisbê, Boeotia,”
linking the ring to a sepulchral treasure from Thisbe in Boeotia and to Minoan 
afterlife imagery.", "source_ids": ["20260628180637996478_4ed0ad3f"], 
"citations": [7]}, {"id": "20260628180640442195_4de7aad3", "type": "Evidence", 
"text": "Boardman’s discussion as summarised notes that “the treasure includes 
multiple gold signet rings and bead seals, situating the Ring of Nestor within a
broader assemblage of engraved Minoan-style objects and emphasizing its 
importance for understanding Minoan religious iconography.”", "source_ids": 
["20260628180637996478_4ed0ad3f"], "citations": [7]}, {"id": 
"20260628180640534411_537a8e47", "type": "Evidence", "text": "Boardman “provides
detailed iconographic analysis of the engraved scene on the Ring of Nestor, 
interpreting it as a complex ritual or afterworld scene with figures, 
structures, and possibly a tree or pillar.”", "source_ids": 
["20260628180637996478_4ed0ad3f"], "citations": [7]}, {"id": 
"20260628180640623176_ef01da80", "type": "Evidence", "text": "Boardman “argues 
that the ring is Minoan (Late Minoan I) in style and iconography, but found in a
mainland Greek (Boeotian) context, raising issues of trade, gift exchange, or 
heirlooms.”", "source_ids": ["20260628180637996478_4ed0ad3f"], "citations": 
[7]}, {"id": "20260628180640715847_e2927469", "type": "Evidence", "text": "The 
ResearchGate abstract notes: “In 1995 the ring was once again placed on display 
in the Ashmolean Museum,” confirming a display event for the Ring of Nestor at 
the Ashmolean.", "source_ids": ["20260628180638033427_842b6b60"], "citations": 
[8]}, {"id": "20260628180640818507_c690f8a4", "type": "Evidence", "text": "The 
ResearchGate description states that the article includes “a comprehensive 
catalogue including all published seals bearing hybrids,” indicating Boardman’s 
systematic treatment of hybrid-bearing seals including the Ring of Nestor.", 
"source_ids": ["20260628180638033427_842b6b60"], "citations": [8]}, {"id": 
"20260628180640920057_bff9604e", "type": "Evidence", "text": "The Nature note 
reports that Sir Arthur Evans “has presented to the Ashmolean Museum, Oxford … 
his unique collection of Minoan sealstones and gems, gold rings and jewels” as 
an accession to the ‘Minoan Room’.", "source_ids": 
["20260628180638068229_6ba8230a"], "citations": [9]}, {"id": 
"20260628180641016664_36db0afc", "type": "Evidence", "text": "Nature highlights 
that “Among the rings is the famous great gold ring of Nestor, the group of gold
beads known as the Treasure of Thisbe, all engraved with scenes of ritual, 
combat and ancient legend,” associating the Ring of Nestor with the Treasure of 
Thisbe and describing their engraved themes.", "source_ids": 
["20260628180638068229_6ba8230a"], "citations": [9]}, {"id": 
"20260628180641112717_274f72a4", "type": "Evidence", "text": "The Nature piece 
characterises Evans’s collection as “far-famed and unrivalled,” and notes that 
the Ashmolean’s Minoan antiquities are “the most important and the most complete
outside Crete itself.”", "source_ids": ["20260628180638068229_6ba8230a"], 
"citations": [9]}, {"id": "20260628180641214476_76a8bd6c", "type": "Evidence", 
"text": "The Academia.edu paper description states that it “presents an analysis
of the iconography surrounding the 'Ring of Nestor' in the context of Aegean 
forgeries and authenticity debates,” indicating that the ring’s authenticity has
been questioned.", "source_ids": ["20260628180627919021_b2e5c47b"], "citations":
[4]}, {"id": "20260628180641312300_f329609e", "type": "Evidence", "text": "The 
same description notes that the study “situates the ring in the context of 
Aegean forgeries,” suggesting that some scholars consider it possibly modern or 
altered rather than a purely Bronze Age artifact.", "source_ids": 
["20260628180627919021_b2e5c47b"], "citations": [4]}, {"id": 
"20260628180641422006_0fbce2ae", "type": "Evidence", "text": "The Academia.edu 
summary indicates that the paper “offers detailed iconographic comparison with 
other Minoan or Mycenaean gold rings and glyptic scenes, focusing on stylistic 
peculiarities of the Ring of Nestor” and “emphasizes methodological issues in 
distinguishing genuine Bronze Age Minoan artifacts from later forgeries in 
museum collections.”", "source_ids": ["20260628180627919021_b2e5c47b"], 
"citations": [4]}, {"id": "20260628180641524842_4e85eb38", "type": "Evidence", 
"text": "The Past article states that “the only other from this area was the 
famous Ring of Nestor – purchased by Arthur Evans,” in reference to gold signet 
rings from the Pylos area.", "source_ids": ["20260628180638137344_cb886f06"], 
"citations": [10]}, {"id": "20260628180641620386_6b204bff", "type": "Evidence", 
"text": "The Past article comments that “Both the Ring of Nestor and the Ring of
Minos have bands strikingly similar to ours, although with disks in the first 
instance rather than …,” using band similarity and the presence of disks on the 
Ring of Nestor as comparative physical features.", "source_ids": 
["20260628180638137344_cb886f06"], "citations": [10]}, {"id": 
"20260628180641728851_adb7e633", "type": "Evidence", "text": "The Past piece 
notes that “the discovery of the Griffin Warrior’s ring revived interest in 
older famous pieces like the Ring of Nestor and the Ring of Minos,” linking the 
Griffin Warrior find to renewed attention on the Ring of Nestor.", "source_ids":
["20260628180638137344_cb886f06"], "citations": [10]}, {"id": 
"20260628180641844675_3d8da285", "type": "Evidence", "text": "The Ashmolean 
Museum’s image library search result lists item “1130 - Signet ring - preferred 
(descriptive) The Ring of Nestor - popular (original language) - Late Minoan I 
Period (c. 1600 - c. 1450 BC),” providing the object’s identifier, type, popular
name, and period dating.", "source_ids": ["20260628180638176961_15c55d96"], 
"citations": [11]}, {"id": "20260628180641946140_2a2147bf", "type": "Evidence", 
"text": "The Ashmolean search result shows that “Signet ring” is the preferred 
descriptive designation and “The Ring of Nestor” is indicated as the popular 
name, reflecting the museum’s cataloguing practice for the object.", 
"source_ids": ["20260628180638176961_15c55d96"], "citations": [11]}, {"id": 
"20260628180642048485_38b18828", "type": "Evidence", "text": "The Ashmolean 
image library entry appears under searches related to Minoan themes and includes
the Ring of Nestor as part of the museum’s imageable collection, implying its 
current location in the Ashmolean Museum.", "source_ids": 
["20260628180638176961_15c55d96"], "citations": [11]}, {"id": 
"20260628180817484475_54845f44", "type": "Claim", "text": "The object popularly 
known as the Ring of Nestor is catalogued in the Ashmolean Museum as a gold 
signet ring (object ID 1130), with the preferred descriptive term 'signet ring' 
and the popular name 'The Ring of Nestor', dated by the museum to the Late 
Minoan I period (c. 1600–1450 BC).", "source_ids": 
["20260628180638176961_15c55d96"], "citations": [11]}, {"id": 
"20260628180817713372_7afc4cf0", "type": "Claim", "text": "Sir Arthur Evans 
donated his collection of Minoan sealstones, gems, gold rings, and jewels, 
including the famous great gold Ring of Nestor and the Treasure of Thisbe, to 
the Ashmolean Museum in Oxford as an accession to its Minoan Room, thereby 
placing the Ring of Nestor within the Ashmolean’s Minoan collection.", 
"source_ids": ["20260628180638068229_6ba8230a"], "citations": [9]}], "offset": 
0, "limit": 20, "total": 25, "next_offset": 20}
