{"query": {"id": "20260628181404869739_998e4ad4", "handle": "q3.1", "text": 
"Resolve conflict: Evans and Childe present the Ring of Nestor as an authentic 
Late Bronze Age artifact providing a glimpse into Minoan afterlife beliefs and 
illustrating Minoan influence on Greek religion, whereas later scholars such as 
Vlachopoulos, the iconographic study on Academia.edu, and Hudler classify the 
ring as a \"Gemma dubitanda\" and question its authenticity, provenance, and 
straightforward cultural attribution.", "importance": "core"}, "items": [{"id": 
"20260628181852192621_a8d08f1d", "type": "Evidence", "text": "Vlachopoulos notes
that the Ring of Nestor \"has long been treated as a 'gemma dubitanda'\" and 
sets out to apply a \"thorough critical approach\" to its authenticity, style 
and iconography within the broader context of problematic early 20th-century 
Aegean glyptic pieces.", "source_ids": ["20260628181849708644_ed58ecf6"], 
"citations": [19]}, {"id": "20260628181852289516_c2ab2896", "type": "Evidence", 
"text": "According to the chapter, Evans’s original publication presented the 
Ring of Nestor as a Late Bronze Age funerary object with Minoan-related 
iconography, whereas subsequent scholars have pointed to \"stylistic anomalies\"
and \"technical features\" that cast doubt on this attribution.", "source_ids": 
["20260628181849708644_ed58ecf6"], "citations": [19]}, {"id": 
"20260628181852401390_36fc805d", "type": "Evidence", "text": "Vlachopoulos 
places the Ring of Nestor among a corpus of \"pseudo-Minoan\" or doubtful 
glyptic artifacts and argues that its unusual combination of motifs, lack of 
secure provenance, and technical concerns warrant maintaining its classification
as a doubtful object rather than as a securely authenticated Late Bronze Age 
piece.", "source_ids": ["20260628181849708644_ed58ecf6"], "citations": [19]}, 
{"id": "20260628181852508751_fe1018c1", "type": "Evidence", "text": "The Journal
of Ancient Egyptian Interconnections article states that the so-called Nestor 
Ring, reportedly found in 1924, \"was accepted as authentic by Sir Arthur Evans 
whereas many other scholars condemned it as a forgery\" and refers to it as 
\"pseudo-Minoan.\"", "source_ids": ["20260628181849748548_38fcfd49"], 
"citations": [20]}, {"id": "20260628181852606566_001c62c7", "type": "Evidence", 
"text": "The article argues that much of the ring’s imagery derives from 
Egyptian models and uses this observation, together with the ring’s uncertain 
provenance, to support its classification among doubtful or forged Aegean 
glyptic objects.", "source_ids": ["20260628181849748548_38fcfd49"], "citations":
[20]}, {"id": "20260628181852712485_7fb03c83", "type": "Evidence", "text": "The 
article warns that the case of the Nestor Ring illustrates the need for caution 
when using unprovenanced artifacts to reconstruct Bronze Age religious beliefs, 
due to the risk of forgery and modern hybrid creations.", "source_ids": 
["20260628181849748548_38fcfd49"], "citations": [20]}, {"id": 
"20260628181852811827_90a6d8f9", "type": "Evidence", "text": "Pini’s abstract 
describes the article as presenting \"new technical observations\" on the Ring 
of Nestor which \"serve to support the authenticity\" of the object, focusing on
aspects such as the goldwork and engraving technique.", "source_ids": 
["20260628181849788871_b2f4c6ca"], "citations": [21]}, {"id": 
"20260628181852932904_0893b1d7", "type": "Evidence", "text": "Pini compares the 
ring’s manufacture with that of securely provenanced Late Bronze Age Aegean gold
signet rings and concludes that certain technical features, including wear and 
tool marks, are more consistent with ancient production than with modern 
forgery.", "source_ids": ["20260628181849788871_b2f4c6ca"], "citations": [21]}, 
{"id": "20260628181853045214_edb8ed82", "type": "Evidence", "text": "The Oxford 
Journal of Archaeology metadata indicates that Pini’s article \"The 'Ring of 
Nestor'\" appears in volume 17, issue 1 (1998), pages 1–13, and that it is a 
substantive study rather than a short note.", "source_ids": 
["20260628180638033427_842b6b60"], "citations": [8]}, {"id": 
"20260628181853165455_9b9038bc", "type": "Evidence", "text": "The ResearchGate 
record summarizes that Pini’s article uses examination of manufacture, wear, and
tool marks to test whether the Ring of Nestor is a modern forgery or a Bronze 
Age original, and that it has been frequently cited in later scholarship on the 
ring’s authenticity.", "source_ids": ["20260628180638033427_842b6b60"], 
"citations": [8]}, {"id": "20260628181853290400_e285d8dc", "type": "Evidence", 
"text": "Evans’s monograph title describes the object as \"The ring of Nestor; a
glimpse into the Minoan after-world, and a sepulchral treasure of gold 
signet-rings and bead-seals from Thisbê, Boeotia,\" explicitly associating the 
ring with a funerary context and Minoan-related afterlife imagery.", 
"source_ids": ["20260628180725501253_8fd75c2b"], "citations": [13]}, {"id": 
"20260628181853402777_d5349a00", "type": "Evidence", "text": "Evans interprets 
the ring’s scene as involving a goddess or divine figure, a tree, and ritual or 
epiphany elements, and uses it to argue for continuity between Minoan religious 
concepts and later Greek beliefs, treating the object as an authentic Late 
Bronze Age Mycenaean-period artifact influenced by Minoan style.", "source_ids":
["20260628180725501253_8fd75c2b"], "citations": [13]}, {"id": 
"20260628181853519463_ff70b18d", "type": "Evidence", "text": "Evans accepts the 
reported discovery of the ring as part of a \"sepulchral treasure\" from Thisbê 
in Boeotia with little critical discussion of potential issues in provenance or 
context.", "source_ids": ["20260628180725501253_8fd75c2b"], "citations": [13]}, 
{"id": "20260628181853627560_2989aaa1", "type": "Evidence", "text": "Eliopoulos 
describes the Ring of Nestor as a \"gemma dubitanda\" and situates his 
iconographic analysis within the broader discussion of Aegean forgeries and 
authenticity debates.", "source_ids": ["20260628180627919021_b2e5c47b"], 
"citations": [4]}, {"id": "20260628181853732385_2c19abb2", "type": "Evidence", 
"text": "Eliopoulos emphasizes that the combination of motifs on the ring, 
including the guardian figure, tree-of-the-world motif, and altar forms, is 
unusual or anomalous when compared with securely provenanced Minoan and 
Mycenaean glyptic.", "source_ids": ["20260628180627919021_b2e5c47b"], 
"citations": [4]}, {"id": "20260628181853835201_0a3e348a", "type": "Evidence", 
"text": "Eliopoulos suggests that the imagery of the Ring of Nestor may reflect 
early 20th-century cultural practices or hybrid artistic reconstructions rather 
than direct transmission of Late Bronze Age religious scenes, and calls for 
cautious use of such unprovenanced artifacts in reconstructing ancient belief 
systems.", "source_ids": ["20260628180627919021_b2e5c47b"], "citations": [4]}, 
{"id": "20260628182035890379_7331c1fc", "type": "Claim", "text": "Arthur Evans 
treated the Ring of Nestor as an authentic Late Bronze Age (Mycenaean-period) 
gold signet ring, reportedly found in a sepulchral context at Thisbê in Boeotia,
whose imagery he interpreted as Minoan-influenced funerary and afterlife scenes 
supporting continuity between Minoan religious concepts and later Greek 
beliefs.", "source_ids": ["20260628180725501253_8fd75c2b", 
"20260628181849708644_ed58ecf6"], "citations": [13, 19]}, {"id": 
"20260628182036297023_71371da5", "type": "Claim", "text": "Modern scholarship is
divided on the authenticity of the Ring of Nestor: Pini’s detailed technical and
gemological study argues that its manufacture, wear, and tool marks are 
consistent with a genuine Late Bronze Age Aegean gold ring, while acknowledging 
its problematic provenance and atypical iconography, whereas other scholars 
classify it as a pseudo‑Minoan ‘gemma dubitanda’ or possible forgery based on 
its uncertain find circumstances, unusual combination of motifs, stylistic links
to Egyptian models, and technical concerns.", "source_ids": 
["20260628180627919021_b2e5c47b", "20260628180638033427_842b6b60", 
"20260628181849708644_ed58ecf6", "20260628181849748548_38fcfd49", 
"20260628181849788871_b2f4c6ca"], "citations": [4, 8, 19, 20, 21]}, {"id": 
"20260628182037060708_2de47e1b", "type": "Claim", "text": "The Ring of Nestor 
lacks secure archaeological provenance and is associated only with a reported 
1924 discovery as part of a sepulchral treasure from Thisbê in Boeotia, a 
circumstance that modern authors highlight as a major reason to treat it 
cautiously or classify it among doubtful or pseudo‑Minoan Aegean glyptic 
objects.", "source_ids": ["20260628180725501253_8fd75c2b", 
"20260628181849708644_ed58ecf6", "20260628181849748548_38fcfd49"], "citations": 
[13, 19, 20]}, {"id": "20260628182037486950_da416155", "type": "Claim", "text": 
"Iconographic studies by Eliopoulos and others argue that the Ring of Nestor’s 
combination of motifs—such as a guardian figure, tree‑of‑the‑world motif, and 
altar forms—differs markedly from securely provenanced Minoan and Mycenaean 
glyptic and shows strong dependence on Egyptian models, which they interpret as 
evidence for a modern or hybrid creation and as a reason to treat the ring with 
caution when reconstructing Bronze Age religion.", "source_ids": 
["20260628180627919021_b2e5c47b", "20260628181849748548_38fcfd49"], "citations":
[4, 20]}], "offset": 0, "limit": 20, "total": 21, "next_offset": 20}
