{"query": {"id": "20260628182156713112_045b093d", "handle": "q3.1.1", "text": 
"Resolve conflict: Evans presents the Ring of Nestor as an authentic Late Bronze
Age Mycenaean-period artifact with Minoan-related afterlife imagery and secure 
funerary provenance from Thisbê, whereas later scholars such as Vlachopoulos and
Eliopoulos classify it as a 'gemma dubitanda' or pseudo-Minoan object with 
problematic provenance and anomalous iconography, and the Journal of Ancient 
Egyptian Interconnections article explicitly treats it as pseudo-Minoan and 
likely modern or hybrid, while Pini’s technical study argues that manufacturing 
features support its authenticity as a Bronze Age object despite the 
questionable context.", "importance": "core"}, "items": [], "offset": 0, 
