{"accepted": true, "revision_requested": false, "severity": "clean", "report": 
"# Ring of Nestor\n\nThe Ring of Nestor is a large gold signet ring, now in the 
Ashmolean Museum in Oxford, that has been linked since the early 20th century to
a reported sepulchral hoard of gold signet rings and bead seals said to have 
been discovered in a Mycenaean rock-cut tomb near Thisbe in Boeotia, central 
Greece, in 1915. Arthur Evans and subsequent museum catalogues have generally 
attributed the ring to the Aegean Bronze Age, styling it Late Minoan I in terms 
of craftsmanship and iconography, while Evans himself argued for a late 
Mycenaean/Late Minoan III date for the tomb on the basis of associated grave 
goods. The ring’s engraved scene, combining ritual figures, structures, a tree 
or pillar, and other motifs, has been interpreted as a complex religious or 
afterlife image reflecting Minoan influence on mainland Greek beliefs, but its 
unusual iconography, art-market provenance, and lack of secure excavation 
context have led modern scholars to classify it as a \"gemma dubitanda\" and to 
debate whether it is an authentic Bronze Age artifact, a pseudo‑Minoan hybrid, 
or a modern forgery. [1][2][12][15][11][7][3][4][14][20]\n\n## Discovery and 
provenance\n\nIn his 1925 study, Arthur Evans described the Ring of Nestor as 
part of a \"royal treasure\" of gold signet rings and bead seals found in a 
Mycenaean rock-cut chamber tomb near the site of Thisbe in Boeotia, close to the
village of Dombrena. Evans reported that the hoard was discovered in 1915 by a 
local peasant in a chamber tomb excavated into the rock and that the assemblage 
included bronze spearheads, a short sword of very late Mycenaean type, a stirrup
jar of late form, glass-paste necklace elements of early Late Minoan IIIb date, 
and other objects. On the basis of these associated finds, he placed the tomb 
and its contents, including the Ring of Nestor, in a late Mycenaean/Late Minoan 
III chronological horizon. [1][15][16]\n\nEvans presented the treasure as a 
sepulchral deposit from a funerary context at Thisbe and interpreted the ring as
offering a \"glimpse into the Minoan after‑world,\" emphasizing connections 
between Minoan religious imagery and mainland Mycenaean funerary practice. The 
HathiTrust catalog record and journal metadata for his article repeat this 
description, situating the work within the Journal of Hellenic Studies and 
associating it with subject headings such as \"Nestor (Greek mythology)\" and 
\"Minoan civilization,\" which reflect Evans’s interpretive framing. 
[1][2][15][13]\n\nLater scholarship has highlighted that the treasure, including
the Ring of Nestor, was acquired through the art market rather than via 
documented archaeological excavation, and that the reported tomb context at 
Thisbe cannot be independently verified. Hudler notes that Evans published a 
group of gold signet rings and bead seals \"allegedly originating\" from a tomb 
at Thisbe, and that this lack of secure provenance has contributed to doubts 
about the ring’s authenticity and dating. The Journal of Ancient Egyptian 
Interconnections article similarly characterizes the ring as \"pseudo‑Minoan\" 
and stresses that its find circumstances are problematic, arguing that 
unprovenanced artifacts such as this should be used cautiously in reconstructing
Bronze Age religious beliefs. [5][4][20][23]\n\nThe Ring of Nestor entered the 
Ashmolean Museum as part of Evans’s donation of his collection of Minoan 
sealstones, gems, gold rings, and jewels to the museum’s Minoan Room. A 
contemporaneous note in Nature describes this gift, stating that among the rings
was \"the famous great gold ring of Nestor\" and the group of gold beads known 
as the \"Treasure of Thisbe,\" all engraved with scenes of ritual, combat, and 
ancient legend. Subsequent Ashmolean publications and catalogues have followed 
Evans in associating the ring with the Treasure of Thisbe, while modern scholars
distinguish between Evans’s narrative of a sepulchral treasure and the 
documented art‑market acquisition. [9][11][22][19]\n\n## Description\n\nThe Ring
of Nestor is a gold signet ring of substantial size; Vlachopoulos reports that 
it measures approximately 3.29 cm by 2.18 cm, making it one of the largest known
Aegean signet rings. The Ashmolean Museum’s image library catalogues the object 
as a gold \"signet ring\" with the popular name \"The Ring of Nestor\" and 
assigns it to the Late Minoan I period (c. 1600–1450 BC) on stylistic grounds. 
The band of the ring is noted in comparative discussions for its similarity to 
those of other well‑known gold rings, such as the so‑called Ring of Minos, 
although the Ring of Nestor has a disk rather than other bezel forms. 
[11][19][10]\n\nThe engraved scene on the bezel is complex and multi‑part. Evans
and later analysts describe a composition featuring a female or divine figure, 
structural elements interpreted as altars or buildings, and a prominent tree or 
pillar motif that has been compared to a \"tree of the world.\" Boardman’s 
discussion emphasizes that the ring’s imagery can be understood as a ritual or 
afterworld scene, with figures and structures arranged in a way that suggests 
religious action or epiphany. Other studies refer to a guardian figure, 
sometimes called Evans’s \"watch‑dog\" or \"dragon,\" situated in relation to 
the tree and altars. [7][4][19]\n\nTechnical examinations have focused on the 
workmanship of the gold and the engraving. Pini’s article in the Oxford Journal 
of Archaeology undertakes a detailed comparison between the Ring of Nestor and 
securely provenanced Late Bronze Age Aegean gold signet rings, analyzing 
features such as tool marks, wear, and the construction of the band and bezel. 
According to the ResearchGate summary, Pini argues that these technical 
characteristics are more consistent with ancient production than with modern 
manufacture, and uses them to support the authenticity of the ring while 
acknowledging the issues posed by its provenance. [8][21]\n\n## Iconography and 
interpretations\n\nEvans interpreted the Ring of Nestor primarily as evidence 
for Minoan afterlife beliefs. In his 1925 monograph, subtitled \"A glimpse into 
the Minoan after‑world,\" he argued that the ring’s imagery—featuring a goddess 
or divine figure, a tree, and ritual elements—illustrated concepts of the soul 
and the afterlife and supported continuity between Minoan religious ideas and 
later Greek beliefs. Childe’s review in Nature framed Evans’s work on the ring 
within a broader argument that classical Greek civilisation was rooted in Minoan
culture and its Mycenaean \"continental daughter,\" thus treating the ring as a 
key piece in tracing these cultural roots. [1][2][13]\n\nBoardman’s iconographic
analysis situates the Ring of Nestor within a wider assemblage of engraved 
Minoan‑style objects, emphasizing its importance for understanding Minoan 
religious iconography. He interprets the engraved scene as a complex ritual or 
afterworld tableau, involving figures, structures, and a tree or pillar, and 
dates the ring stylistically to Late Minoan I, while noting that its reported 
findspot in Boeotia raises questions about the movement of such objects between 
Crete and the mainland through trade, gift exchange, or heirloom transmission. 
[7][11]\n\nEliopoulos’s study, \"Some Observations on the Iconography of the 
‘Ring of Nestor’,\" approaches the ring in the context of Aegean forgeries and 
authenticity debates. He examines specific motifs, including the guardian figure
(Evans’s \"watch‑dog\" or \"dragon\"), incurved altars, and the so‑called tree 
of the world, and compares them with parallels in verified Minoan glyptic and 
wall paintings. Eliopoulos argues that the combination of these elements on a 
single ring is unusual or anomalous when compared with securely provenanced Late
Bronze Age Aegean material, and suggests that aspects of the imagery may reflect
early 20th‑century cultural practices or hybrid artistic reconstructions rather 
than straightforward Bronze Age religious scenes. [4]\n\nThe Journal of Ancient 
Egyptian Interconnections article, \"The Pseudo‑Minoan Nestor Ring and Its 
Egyptian Iconography,\" further emphasizes the ring’s iconographic anomalies. It
states that the Nestor Ring, reportedly found in the 1920s, was accepted as 
authentic by Evans but condemned as a forgery by many other scholars, and 
characterizes it as \"pseudo‑Minoan\". The authors argue that much of the ring’s
imagery derives from Egyptian models, and they use this dependence on Egyptian 
iconography, together with the ring’s uncertain provenance, to support its 
classification among doubtful or forged Aegean glyptic objects. They caution 
that such unprovenanced and hybrid artifacts should not be used uncritically to 
reconstruct Bronze Age religious beliefs. [20][23]\n\nIn contrast, Pini and some
later authors emphasize that iconographic anomalies alone cannot demonstrate 
forgery. Pini’s technical study argues that the ring’s manufacture, wear, and 
tool marks are consistent with ancient production, and that certain iconographic
features, such as the representation of incurved altars and specific 
arrangements of figures and trees, can be paralleled in authentic Aegean Bronze 
Age material. Vlachopoulos likewise notes that structural and stylistic 
comparisons support treating the ring as a Late Bronze Age artifact, even while 
he maintains that its lack of secure archaeological context requires it to 
remain in the category of \"gemma dubitanda\". [8][21][19]\n\n## Authenticity 
and dating debate\n\nFrom its first publication, the Ring of Nestor has been the
subject of debate over its authenticity and dating. Evans treated it as an 
authentic Late Bronze Age Mycenaean‑period gold signet ring, found in a funerary
context at Thisbe in Boeotia, whose imagery he interpreted as Minoan‑influenced 
funerary and afterlife scenes. He dated the tomb to a late Mycenaean/Late Minoan
III horizon based on the associated grave goods, while recognising that the 
ring’s style aligned more closely with Minoan glyptic traditions. 
[1][13][15]\n\nModern scholarship has divided over whether the ring is genuinely
ancient. Pini’s article \"The 'Ring of Nestor'\" presents new technical 
observations and concludes that manufacturing features such as the construction 
of the band and bezel, tool marks, and patterns of wear are consistent with a 
Bronze Age origin rather than a modern forgery. Vlachopoulos’s chapter \"The 
Ring of Nestor and the Quest for Authenticity\" likewise reports that research 
on craftsmanship, metal composition, iconography, and comparative material 
\"confirms its authenticity\" and notes that the ring’s dimensions make it the 
fifth‑largest known Aegean signet ring. These authors nevertheless stress that 
the absence of secure excavation data means the ring should be treated as a 
doubtful object in terms of provenance, even if they regard it as ancient in 
manufacture. [8][21][19]\n\nOther scholars have been more sceptical. Hudler’s 
study on \"e‑scaping arguments\" in digital archaeology lists the Ring of Nestor
among \"gemmae dubitandae\" (doubtful gems) and recounts that at least one 
scholar writing contemporaneously with the ring’s appearance in the 1920s 
explicitly condemned it as a modern forgery. Eliopoulos describes the ring as a 
\"gemma dubitanda\" and situates his iconographic analysis amid broader debates 
on Aegean forgeries, arguing that its collage‑like combination of motifs and 
possible reflection of 1920s cultural practices justify continued doubt. The 
Journal of Ancient Egyptian Interconnections article goes further, explicitly 
treating the ring as pseudo‑Minoan and using its Egyptian stylistic borrowings 
and art‑market provenance to support a classification as a likely modern or 
hybrid creation. [5][4][20]\n\nThe Cambridge chapter \"Fakes and Dubitanda\" in 
The Art and Archaeology of the Aegean Bronze Age reflects this divided 
assessment. It notes that although the Ring of Nestor is considered in that 
context to be authentic, doubts surrounding it \"have not ceased and probably 
never will,\" and places it within a category of artifacts whose status remains 
debated. Tsangaraki’s discussion of a demonstrably modern forged gold signet 
ring at the Archaeological Museum of Thessaloniki uses that case to show how 
modern forgers borrow and misapply Minoan–Mycenaean imagery and forms, and 
explicitly cites the debate over the Ring of Nestor as an example of the 
challenges involved in deciding whether unprovenanced glyptic pieces belong in 
Minoan archaeology. [14][24]\n\nDating proposals for the Ring of Nestor reflect 
these authenticity concerns. The Ashmolean Museum dates the ring stylistically 
to the Late Minoan I period (c. 1600–1450 BC), aligning it with early mature 
phases of Minoan glyptic. Evans, focusing on the tomb assemblage, argued for a 
late Mycenaean/Late Minoan III date for the burial as a whole. Pini and 
Vlachopoulos generally follow a Bronze Age attribution while acknowledging 
uncertainty over precise phase, whereas scholars who consider the ring a forgery
regard its chronological horizon as 20th‑century, linked to the period of its 
appearance on the art market. [11][15][8][21][20]\n\n## Reception and 
influence\n\nSince its publication, the Ring of Nestor has played a prominent 
role in discussions of Aegean Bronze Age glyptic, religious iconography, and the
problem of archaeological forgeries. Evans’s original study and Childe’s review 
brought the ring to wide attention as part of a sepulchral treasure from Thisbe 
and as a key piece illustrating Minoan afterlife beliefs and their influence on 
Greek religion. Boardman incorporated the ring into a comprehensive catalogue of
seals bearing hybrid creatures, using it to explore Minoan religious imagery and
treating it as an important object within the Ashmolean’s collection. 
[1][2][7][8]\n\nDebates over the ring’s authenticity have made it a recurring 
case study in scholarship on Aegean forgeries. Eliopoulos’s iconographic paper, 
Hudler’s digital archaeology study, Vlachopoulos’s chapter on the \"quest for 
authenticity,\" and the Journal of Ancient Egyptian Interconnections article all
use the Ring of Nestor to illustrate methodological challenges in distinguishing
genuine Bronze Age artifacts from modern pseudo‑Minoan creations and in handling
doubtful objects in museum collections and online corpora. Tsangaraki’s analysis
of a forged Minoan‑Mycenaean‑type ring similarly cites the Nestor Ring debate as
part of a wider discourse on how forgers select and combine motifs. 
[3][4][5][20][19][24]\n\nThe ring has also featured in broader public and 
scholarly discussions of Aegean gold signet rings. A feature in The Past on the 
Griffin Warrior tomb at Pylos notes that prior to the discovery of the Griffin 
Warrior’s rings, the only other gold signet ring known from that area was the 
\"famous Ring of Nestor,\" purchased by Evans, and comments that the new finds 
revived interest in older pieces such as the Rings of Nestor and Minos. Popular 
and institutional articles on the Griffin Warrior emphasize how gold rings with 
Minoan‑style imagery, including those from Pylos, connect mainland Mycenaean 
elites with Minoan religious symbolism and with Homeric figures such as Nestor, 
thereby reinforcing the conceptual landscape in which the Ring of Nestor is 
discussed. [10][17][18]\n\nWithin the Ashmolean Museum, the Ring of Nestor has 
been exhibited and re‑displayed as part of its Minoan collections. The 
ResearchGate abstract notes that the ring was placed on display again in the 
Ashmolean in 1995, and museum image library entries show that it remains part of
the institution’s imageable holdings, catalogued under its object number and 
Late Minoan I period designation. These displays have contributed to the ring’s 
visibility in both specialist literature and public presentations of Aegean 
Bronze Age art. [8][11]\n\n## References\n\n[1] Evans, Arthur J.: \"The ring of 
Nestor\". A glimpse into the Minoan after-world. Sepulchral treasure of gold 
signet-rings and bead-seals from Thisbê, Boeotia 
